// The EDGE estate's ONE Renovate policy. A PUBLIC repo's renovate.json is a single line:
//   { "extends": ["github>mjdevaccount/ci-kit//renovate/default.json5"] }
// A PRIVATE repo's still says `github>mjdevaccount/edge-ci//renovate/default.json5`, and that
// file is itself one line extending this one. Either way a rule changes here, once, for all.
//
// WHY HERE AND NOT edge-ci (2026-11-03). This file lived in edge-ci until then. edge-ci is
// PRIVATE, and Renovate gives a public repo a token scoped to that repo alone, so it cannot read
// a preset in a private one (docs.renovatebot.com/getting-started/private-packages/). llm-ports
// (public) opened #24 "Cannot find preset's package (github>mjdevaccount/edge-ci//...)" and
// stopped opening PRs; ci-kit (public) extended the same path. ci-kit is public, so every repo
// can read it. ONE copy: edge-ci's file is a pointer, never a second policy.
//
// WHY RENOVATE (2026-10-10). Dependency refreshes were a session's manual work: a lock refresh,
// a CVE bump, "held for operator call" lists that nobody revisited. The fleet collectors KNEW
// the boxes were behind and nothing turned that into a change. Renovate turns "a newer version
// exists" into a PR, and CI -- the merge gate everywhere -- decides whether it lands.
//
// THE MERGE GATE IS STILL CI -- EVERY CHECK, NOT ONLY THE REQUIRED ONES. GitHub's own auto-merge
// (`platformAutomerge`) waits only on the checks branch protection REQUIRES, and the checks that
// prove a dependency bump (trade-data's PR-time image build, mattpc-llm-stack's requirements
// resolve) are not required: the Claude app that runs sessions has no Administration permission,
// so branch protection stays a hand click. With platformAutomerge OFF Renovate merges itself, and
// only once every check run on the PR is green (`ignoreTests` is false; a skipped step is a real
// green). Nothing here merges past a red check, required or not. Cost: the merge lands on
// Renovate's next pass after the last check reports, not the second it does.
{
  $schema: "https://docs.renovatebot.com/renovate-schema.json",
  extends: [
    "config:recommended",
    ":semanticCommits",
    ":dependencyDashboard",      // one issue per repo: everything pending, deferred, blocked
    ":rebaseStalePrs",
  ],
  timezone: "America/New_York",  // market logic is ET; so is the window below
  // Weekend only: a merge in trade-infra / trade-data DEPLOYS (deploy-reconcile, hourly), and
  // the weekend is the window where nothing trades. BOTH weekend days (2026-10-23): with a
  // Saturday-only window plus the 3-day age wait, a release after Wednesday noon slipped a week.
  // ALL OF SATURDAY, SUNDAY UNTIL 18:00 ET (2026-10-24, trade-infra#515). The window is a filter
  // on runs Mend starts, not a run of its own: Mend runs a "hot" repo (one that has merged a
  // Renovate PR) hourly but a "cold" one -- never merged one -- about ONCE A DAY. trade-quant was
  // onboarded 10-10, had no in-window run on Saturday 10-17, and so opened ZERO PRs in two
  // weeks; zero merges kept it cold, and cold kept it missing the window. Two 13-hour mornings
  // are not guaranteed to contain a once-a-day run; a 24-hour Saturday is. Sunday stops at 18:00
  // so the last auto-merge deploys (deploy-reconcile, hourly) hours before Monday's open.
  schedule: ["* * * * 6", "* 0-17 * * 0"],
  // No day-0 releases: a broken or hijacked publish is usually yanked within hours.
  minimumReleaseAge: "3 days",
  internalChecksFilter: "strict",
  prConcurrentLimit: 15,
  prHourlyLimit: 0,
  labels: ["deps"],
  platformAutomerge: false,   // see the header: Renovate waits on EVERY check, then merges
  // A relock commit is still Renovate's branch. Renovate will not auto-merge (or rebase) a branch
  // carrying a commit it did not author, and the relock workflows (edge-ci relock-uv.yml, local-mcp
  // relock.yml) commit as github-actions[bot] -- local-mcp#221 sat CLEAN, every check green,
  // never merged (#228 added this there; with it #350/#352 merged themselves, 2026-11-01). Here,
  // once, so a newly wired repo cannot forget it. Only that bot: a human's commit on a Renovate
  // branch still stops the auto-merge, which is the point of the rule.
  gitIgnoredAuthors: ["41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com"],

  // Security fixes skip both the window and the age wait.
  vulnerabilityAlerts: {
    enabled: true,
    schedule: ["at any time"],
    minimumReleaseAge: null,
    automerge: true,
    labels: ["deps", "security"],
  },
  osvVulnerabilityAlerts: true,

  // The transitive refresh a session used to do by hand (`uv lock --upgrade`, npm audit fix).
  // NO AGE WAIT HERE (2026-10-31): a lock refresh delegates to the package manager, so the
  // packages it moves carry no release timestamp, and with the default
  // minimumReleaseAgeBehaviour (timestamp-required) the `renovate/stability-days` status stays
  // PENDING FOR AS LONG AS minimumReleaseAge IS SET -- Renovate's own words
  // (docs/usage/key-concepts/minimum-release-age.md, "lockFileMaintenance updates"; its
  // security:minimumReleaseAge* presets null it for lock file maintenance for that reason).
  // Measured: six lock-file PRs (trade-data#2566, trade-quant#93, trade-ui#756, finance-ops#94,
  // edge-ci#40, TradingEdge.Scraper#131) sat 7 days with every CI check green and ONLY
  // stability-days pending; every rebase after a main merge regenerated the lock and the wait
  // began again. A wait that cannot end is a queue, not a gate. The age guard for a lock
  // refresh belongs to the package manager (npm --before / uv exclude-newer; renovate#41652),
  // not to a status that reads a timestamp no lock entry has. CI still gates the merge.
  lockFileMaintenance: {
    enabled: true,
    schedule: ["* * * * 6", "* 0-17 * * 0"],
    automerge: true,
    minimumReleaseAge: null,
  },

  // ---- first-party kit pins Renovate's own managers cannot see (2026-11-01) ----------------
  // ci-kit's ci-pin-drift (pin-drift.yml, a daily cron in each repo) DETECTS a pin older than its
  // kit's newest vX.Y.Z tag; Renovate is the ACTOR that should bump it. Until this block no
  // manager parsed the shapes those pins take outside `uses:`, so every kit release turned
  // pin-drift red and nothing opened a PR: trade-ui (ci-kit v1.7.1 vs v1.10.2), trade-infra and
  // home-host (edge-platform v0.4.0 vs v0.5.0), finance-ops (llm-ports v0.4.0 vs v0.6.0) sat red
  // 2-8 days and were bumped by hand (trade-ui#788, trade-infra#787, home-host#140, finance-ops#110).
  // The shapes are the ones ci_kit/pin_drift.py `pin_re` reads -- change one, change the other.
  //   - `git+https://github.com/mjdevaccount/<kit>(.git)?@vX.Y.Z`: npm scripts (uvx --from), `run:`
  //     pip lines, PEP 723 headers, PowerShell. The `https://github.com/` prefix is REQUIRED so a
  //     `uses: mjdevaccount/<kit>@vX.Y.Z` stays with the github-actions manager, not two managers.
  //     Renovate's own pep621/pep723 managers do not cover these: their shared PEP 508 parser marks
  //     a direct-URL dependency `skipReason: invalid-value` (renovate 42.99.0 dry-run, 2026-11-01),
  //     so the regex below is the only manager that reads them -- no double PR.
  //   - `.../mjdevaccount/<kit>/releases/download/vX.Y.Z/<stem>-X.Y.Z-...` (a wheel no index
  //     publishes). The version is there TWICE; Renovate replaces currentValue everywhere inside
  //     the match (autoReplaceGlobalMatch, default true), so both move together. github-releases,
  //     not -tags: a tag whose release asset is not built yet would be a 404 URL.
  //   - a `.pin` file written `<kit>@vX.Y.Z` (home-host/observer/edge-platform.pin). A .pin that
  //     holds a BARE tag with the kit only in its file name (edge-host/system/ci-kit.pin,
  //     edge-host/system/edge-platform.pin, mcp/scripts/edge-platform.pin) is NOT read here --
  //     nor by ci-pin-drift; write it `<kit>@vX.Y.Z` to bring it under both.
  // Every version must END the pin (`(?:[^\w.-]|$)`, consumed: RE2 has no lookahead), so
  // `@v1.2.3-rc1` is not read as 1.2.3 -- pin_re's `(?![\w.-])`.
  // depName is `mjdevaccount/<kit>` in every shape -- the same name the github-actions manager
  // gives a `uses:` -- so a kit release is ONE branch across workflows, scripts and pins (the
  // packageRule "first-party kits" below keeps the actions group from splitting it). Exception:
  // a uv git source (trade-data `ci-kit = { git = ..., tag = ... }`) is pep621's, and the later
  // "python (non-major)" group wins for it -- a second PR, which pep621 DOES relock.
  // RELOCKED IN ACTIONS, NOT HERE: a regex manager rewrites pyproject.toml and leaves uv.lock
  // stale, and hosted Renovate runs no postUpgradeTasks. A uv repo whose kit pin only a regex
  // reads -- a release WHEEL (finance-ops, trade-quant, trade-data: slim images with no git, so not
  // a uv git source) or edge-ci's PEP 508 `ci-kit @ git+` (pip / pre-commit installs ignore uv
  // sources, and `ci-kit` is unclaimed on PyPI) -- calls edge-ci's reusable `relock-uv.yml`
  // (2026-11-01): on a renovate/ branch it runs `uv lock` and pushes uv.lock with PR_UPDATE_TOKEN so
  // CI re-runs; `gitIgnoredAuthors` (top of this file) lets Renovate still auto-merge that branch.
  // A dev-only uv GIT source in a repo nothing pip-installs (trade-data's ci-kit) needs no
  // workflow: pep621 relocks it (trade-data#2694). Until a repo is wired AND holds the token, its
  // kit bump goes RED and waits -- finance-ops and trade-quant CI run
  // `uv sync --locked`, trade-data and edge-ci `uv lock --check`. A repo that pins a kit in
  // pyproject without one of those must add it first, or a stale lock merges green.
  // A pin with NO lock beside it (edge-host ci.yml's work-timeline wheel, a `run:` pip line)
  // auto-merges on green, so it can move ahead of the same kit in a uv repo still waiting on a
  // relock; nothing checks that two repos' pins agree.
  // uv.lock is never in managerFilePatterns: editing a URL there without its hash breaks the lock.
  customManagers: [
    {
      description: "first-party kit pinned by git URL tag (@vX.Y.Z) outside a `uses:` line",
      customType: "regex",
      managerFilePatterns: [
        "/(^|/)package\\.json$/",
        "/(^|/)pyproject\\.toml$/",
        "/^\\.github/workflows/[^/]+\\.ya?ml$/",
        "/\\.(py|sh|bash|ps1|psm1)$/",
        "/(^|/)(Docker|Container)file[^/]*$/",
      ],
      matchStrings: [
        "https://github\\.com/(?<depName>mjdevaccount/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+?)(?:\\.git)?@v(?<currentValue>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)(?:[^\\w.-]|$)",
      ],
      datasourceTemplate: "github-tags",
      versioningTemplate: "semver",
      extractVersionTemplate: "^v(?<version>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)$",
    },
    {
      description: "first-party kit pinned by GitHub release-asset URL (version in tag AND file name)",
      customType: "regex",
      managerFilePatterns: [
        "/(^|/)pyproject\\.toml$/",
        "/^\\.github/workflows/[^/]+\\.ya?ml$/",
        "/\\.(py|sh|bash|ps1|psm1)$/",
        "/(^|/)(Docker|Container)file[^/]*$/",
      ],
      matchStrings: [
        "https://github\\.com/(?<depName>mjdevaccount/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)/releases/download/v(?<currentValue>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)/[A-Za-z0-9_.]+-\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+-",
      ],
      datasourceTemplate: "github-releases",
      versioningTemplate: "semver",
      extractVersionTemplate: "^v(?<version>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)$",
    },
    {
      description: "first-party kit named in a .pin file as <kit>@vX.Y.Z (owner implied)",
      customType: "regex",
      managerFilePatterns: ["/\\.pin$/"],
      matchStrings: ["(?<kit>[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)@v(?<currentValue>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)(?:[^\\w.-]|$)"],
      depNameTemplate: "mjdevaccount/{{{kit}}}",
      datasourceTemplate: "github-tags",
      versioningTemplate: "semver",
      extractVersionTemplate: "^v(?<version>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)$",
    },
    {
      // THIRD-PARTY release binaries a script pins by hand (2026-11-03, ci-kit v1.11.0: jscpd and
      // oasdiff in ci_kit/*.py). The standard Renovate annotation on the line ABOVE the version:
      //     # renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=oasdiff/oasdiff
      //     OASDIFF_VERSION = "1.35.0"
      // datasource and depName come from the comment, so one manager covers any tool and source.
      // A sha256 table beside the version is NOT moved by Renovate: the bump PR fails the tool's own
      // selftest on the digest (exit 2) until a session moves the hashes in the same PR. That is
      // deliberate -- no binary runs on a digest nobody read. ci-kit tests/
      // test_renovate_annotation_must_fire.py proves this pattern reads the shape and only it.
      description: "third-party version pinned under a `# renovate: datasource=... depName=...` comment",
      customType: "regex",
      managerFilePatterns: [
        "/\\.(py|sh|bash|ps1|psm1|mjs)$/",
        "/^\\.github/workflows/[^/]+\\.ya?ml$/",
      ],
      matchStrings: [
        "#\\s*renovate:\\s*datasource=(?<datasource>[a-z-]+)\\s+depName=(?<depName>[^\\s]+)(?:\\s+versioning=(?<versioning>[^\\s]+))?[^\\n]*\\n[^\\n]*?[\"']?v?(?<currentValue>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)(?:[^\\w.-]|$)",
      ],
      versioningTemplate: "{{#if versioning}}{{{versioning}}}{{else}}semver-coerced{{/if}}",
      extractVersionTemplate: "^v?(?<version>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)$",
    },
  ],

  packageRules: [
    // ---- default: non-major lands on green, major waits for review ------------------------
    {
      matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch", "pin", "digest", "pinDigest"],
      automerge: true,
    },
    {
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      automerge: false,
      labels: ["deps", "major"],
    },

    // ---- a CI action's major lands on green, because CI RUNS IT ----------------------------
    // Measured 2026-10-17: 12 of 19 open Renovate PRs across trade-data + trade-infra were
    // action majors (checkout v7, cache v6, setup-uv v10, docker/* ...), every one CLEAN, none
    // read -- "major waits for review" with no reviewer is a queue, not a gate. An action's
    // major is exercised by the very PR that bumps it: if checkout v7 breaks, the PR goes RED
    // and the every-check rule above refuses it. That holds only for an action whose failure
    // is LOUD. The exceptions below are the ones whose failure is a GREEN.
    {
      matchManagers: ["github-actions"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      automerge: true,
    },
    // ...except an action that DECIDES WHETHER OTHER CHECKS RUN (a broken paths-filter skips
    // the unit job, and a skipped step is a real green), or that IS a guard (a gitleaks that
    // scans nothing passes). Their failure looks exactly like success, so a human reads them.
    {
      matchManagers: ["github-actions"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      matchPackageNames: ["dorny/paths-filter", "gitleaks/gitleaks-action"],
      automerge: false,
      labels: ["deps", "major", "fails-green"],
    },

    // ---- grouping: one PR per ecosystem per week, not forty -------------------------------
    { matchManagers: ["github-actions"], matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch", "digest", "pinDigest"], groupName: "github actions" },
    // First-party kits: NOT in the actions group. A kit release is one branch per kit, keyed on
    // depName `mjdevaccount/<kit>`, holding its `uses:` lines AND the customManagers pins above --
    // grouped into "github actions" the `uses:` half would land apart from the rest, and
    // pin-drift reads every shape. (The python group on the next line still wins for a pep621
    // uv git source of a kit -- see customManagers.)
    // ANY DAY, NO AGE WAIT (2026-11-03, edge-ci#7: ci-kit v1.11.0 "Awaiting Schedule" on a
    // Tuesday while ci-pin-drift stayed red estate-wide). We publish these, so there is no
    // yanked or hijacked upstream to wait out, and deploy-reconcile is already RTH-fenced, which
    // covers the deploy risk the weekend window exists for. No later rule sets `schedule`.
    {
      description: "first-party kits",
      matchPackageNames: ["/^mjdevaccount\\//i"],
      groupName: null,
      schedule: ["at any time"],
      minimumReleaseAge: null,
    },
    { matchManagers: ["pep621", "pip_requirements"], matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch"], groupName: "python (non-major)" },
    { matchManagers: ["npm"], matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch"], groupName: "npm (non-major)" },
    { matchManagers: ["nuget"], matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch"], groupName: "nuget (non-major)" },
    { matchManagers: ["dockerfile"], groupName: "dockerfile base images" },
    // First-party packages PUBLISHED TO A REGISTRY, matched by source repo, not name (2026-11-04).
    // finance-ops#116 moved llm-ports from a release-wheel URL (depName `mjdevaccount/llm-ports`,
    // caught by "first-party kits" above) to PyPI (depName `llm-ports`), and the rule stopped
    // matching: v1.1.0 sat "Pending Status Checks" behind the 3-day age wait inside the weekly
    // "python (non-major)" group while ci-pin-drift was red on the day it shipped. The pypi/npm
    // datasources read the package's repository URL, so this follows a kit to ANY registry under
    // ANY name. AFTER the grouping rules on purpose: a later packageRule wins, and the python
    // group would otherwise fold a kit bump into the weekly PR.
    {
      description: "first-party packages on a registry (llm-ports on PyPI)",
      matchSourceUrls: ["https://github.com/mjdevaccount/**"],
      groupName: null,
      schedule: ["at any time"],
      minimumReleaseAge: null,
    },

    // ---- container images in compose files DEPLOY ON MERGE ---------------------------------
    // trade-infra's compose is shipped by deploy-reconcile within the hour, unattended.
    {
      matchManagers: ["docker-compose"],
      groupName: "compose images",
      labels: ["deps", "deploys-on-merge"],
    },
    // A compose MINOR lands on green too (2026-10-23, trade-infra#515). Until then a minor
    // waited for review, and with one operator "waits for review" meant the Dependency Dashboard
    // grew and nothing landed. Most compose minors are observability (Prometheus, Loki, Tempo,
    // Grafana): a bad bump costs telemetry, not trading data. The stateful exceptions are held
    // by LATER rules -- "database images" below, and the langfuse / windmill rules at the end --
    // so this rule sits HERE, before them, rather than at the end where it would win over them.
    {
      matchManagers: ["docker-compose"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["minor"],
      automerge: true,
    },
    // A compose MAJOR is never grouped: each one is its own architecture decision, and a group
    // makes whoever merges one ship them all. Measured 2026-10-18: trade-infra#611 bundled
    // Langfuse 2 -> 3 (ClickHouse/Redis/S3, owned by the AI-suite thread) with Tempo 2 -> 3,
    // which OBSERVABILITY-TRACING.md records as deliberately NOT taken. The Langfuse merge
    // would have deployed Tempo 3 unattended within the hour. One PR per major, each read
    // on its own. (A database image's major stays in "database images" below: later rule wins.)
    {
      matchManagers: ["docker-compose"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      groupName: null,
    },
    // A FLOATING tag pinned by digest (litellm:main-stable@sha256:...) has no version in the
    // tag, so every move is a "digest" update to Renovate -- and digest updates auto-merge
    // above. Measured 2026-10-10: the box ran litellm 1.91.1 behind that tag while upstream
    // was 1.104.2, so the first Renovate pass would have auto-landed thirteen minor releases
    // of the LLM gateway (config.yaml schema included) as one unattended digest bump. A
    // digest move on a floating tag is a release, not a rebuild; it waits for review.
    {
      matchManagers: ["docker-compose"],
      matchPackageNames: ["ghcr.io/berriai/litellm"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["digest"],
      automerge: false,
      labels: ["deps", "deploys-on-merge", "floating-tag"],
    },

    // ---- the review gate's own action never auto-merges -----------------------------------
    // trade-data's `review gate` (review-gate.yml) is a required check that is GRADED BY THE
    // EDITED WORKFLOW: a PR that changes review-gate.yml is judged by the version it carries.
    // CLAUDE.md names that hole for human PRs; Renovate would make it recurring, because the
    // action's SHA bump rides the auto-merging "github actions" group and lands in that file.
    // So the pin on the gate's action is the one actions update a human reads first.
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["anthropics/claude-code-action"],
      automerge: false,
      labels: ["deps", "review-gate"],
    },

    // Dockerfile bumps are NOT held, and that is safe ONLY in a repo whose CI builds its images
    // on the PR -- with platformAutomerge off above, that check gates the merge whether or not
    // branch protection requires it. THIS PRESET IS SHARED, so the claim is per repo. Until
    // 2026-10-17 it rested on trade-data's image-pr.yml alone: trade-infra built nothing, and its
    // #608 (python:3.14) auto-merged green, failed the first build on the box and wedged
    // deploy-reconcile for 13h. Repos on this preset that hold a Dockerfile, and their PR build:
    //   trade-data   image-pr.yml `image-pr-build`        trade-infra  ci.yml `images` (required)
    //   trade-quant  ci.yml `image-build`                 trade-ui     ci.yml `image-build`
    //   finance-ops  ci.yml (build-push-action)
    //   TradingEdge.Scraper  NONE -- built post-merge by Cloud Build (cd.yml). Not exposed today
    //     only because its bases float on `dotnet/*:10.0` with no digest, so the sole Renovate
    //     update is a major, which its own renovate.json turns off (#133: the move is a TFM
    //     retarget, not an image bump). Pinning a digest there arms the auto-merge.
    // A new repo with a Dockerfile that extends this preset adds its PR build FIRST, or Renovate
    // auto-merges an image nobody built.

    // ---- the database is never automatic ---------------------------------------------------
    // A Postgres / Timescale image change is a dba window (pg_upgrade, extension versions,
    // pgbackrest compatibility), never a Saturday auto-merge.
    {
      matchPackageNames: [
        "timescale/timescaledb-ha",
        "postgres",
        "edoburu/pgbouncer",
        "timescale/pgai-vectorizer-worker",
        "woblerr/pgbackrest_exporter",
      ],
      groupName: "database images",
      automerge: false,
      labels: ["deps", "dba"],
    },
    {
      // pg18.4-ts2.28.3-all: Postgres major/minor, then the Timescale version.
      matchPackageNames: ["timescale/timescaledb-ha"],
      versioning: "regex:^pg(?<major>\\d+)\\.(?<minor>\\d+)-ts(?<patch>\\d+)\\.(?<build>\\d+)\\.(?<revision>\\d+)-(?<compatibility>.+)$",
    },

    // ---- single-operator self-drive (2026-10-23, trade-infra#515) --------------------------
    // These come LAST on purpose: in Renovate a later matching rule wins, so each one below
    // overrides the broad groups and auto-merge defaults above.

    // Langfuse is STATEFUL: it runs Prisma and ClickHouse migrations at startup, so a bump is
    // a one-way schema change. One PR for the four images it moves with, read before merging.
    {
      matchPackageNames: [
        "langfuse/langfuse",
        "langfuse/langfuse-worker",
        "clickhouse/clickhouse-server",
        "redis",
      ],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["minor", "patch", "digest"],
      groupName: "langfuse stack",
      automerge: false,
      labels: ["deps", "deploys-on-merge", "stateful"],
    },
    // Windmill server (compose) and workers (worker.Dockerfile FROM) MUST share one version, so
    // one group spans both managers. It is held for a held-deploy anyway, so a ghcr release with
    // no timestamp must not also sit behind minimumReleaseAge forever.
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["ghcr.io/windmill-labs/windmill"],
      groupName: "windmill",
      automerge: false,
      labels: ["deps", "held-deploy"],
      minimumReleaseAgeBehaviour: "timestamp-optional",
    },
    // A Python base-image bump changes the interpreter under every image built on it (#608:
    // python:3.14 auto-merged and wedged deploy-reconcile for 13h). Its own PR, read first.
    // NO AGE WAIT HERE EITHER (2026-10-31, trade-infra#515): the official `python:X.Y-slim` tag is
    // REBUILT every time Debian slim moves (3.15-slim: created 2026-10-28, ~weekly), and Renovate's
    // docker datasource reads the rebuild's `created` as the release timestamp -- so a 3-day
    // minimumReleaseAge RE-ARMS on every rebuild and the update sits in "Pending Status Checks"
    // with no branch and no PR (trade-infra's dashboard, Friday 2026-10-30: python 3.15 pending,
    // 02:00Z 10-31 it cleared, and the next rebuild would have re-pended it). The age guard is
    // yank protection for an UNATTENDED merge; this rule never auto-merges, and every repo on it
    // builds the image on the PR (the table above), so the wait only hides the PR a session must
    // read. The weekend window still applies.
    {
      matchManagers: ["dockerfile"],
      matchPackageNames: ["python"],
      groupName: "python base image",
      automerge: false,
      minimumReleaseAge: null,
    },
    // A pgbouncer PATCH lands on green: CI runs scripts/test_pgbouncer_lan.sh against the
    // image, so a broken patch is a red check. After "database images" so it wins for patches.
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["edoburu/pgbouncer"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["patch"],
      groupName: "pgbouncer",
      automerge: true,
    },
    // langfuse-db is the Langfuse v2 ROLLBACK database (postgres:17, nothing writes it now). It
    // never moves to 18 -- trade-infra#613 was closed for exactly that.
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["postgres"],
      matchCurrentValue: "/^17/",
      allowedVersions: "<18",
    },
    // Redis stays on 7 until the Langfuse thread picks Redis 8 or the valkey already in the
    // stack. Redis 8 also changed its license.
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["redis"],
      allowedVersions: "<8",
    },
    // ClickHouse follows the series LANGFUSE recommends, not ClickHouse's newest (2026-10-30). It
    // exists here only as Langfuse v4's store, and Langfuse documents "25.12 minimum, 26.4
    // recommended" (langfuse-docs self-hosting/upgrade/upgrade-guides/upgrade-v3-to-v4.mdx)
    // while its own docker-compose.yml still pins 25.12 at v4.72.0. trade-infra#698 grouped
    // 26.4.5 -> 26.10.3: past the 26.8 LTS and 26.9's mandatory analyzer, after Langfuse had
    // already broken on 26.6's analyzer (langfuse#15125, #15977) and on 26.8's DateTime64 parsing
    // (#16858). ClickHouse does not promise a downgrade, so a bad jump is a restore, not a revert.
    // Raise this cap when Langfuse's recommended series moves -- in the same PR that cites it.
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["clickhouse/clickhouse-server"],
      allowedVersions: "<26.5",
    },

    // ---- first-party packages skip the age wait (2026-10-24) -------------------------------
    // minimumReleaseAge guards against a THIRD party's broken or hijacked publish being yanked
    // within hours. Our own tags (ci-kit, edge-ci, a sibling repo pinned by commit) are cut by
    // the same sessions that consume them, and a bad one is fixed by the next tag, not a yank.
    // The wait also never ENDS for some of them: a git-refs pin (local-mcp's .github/scraper.ref
    // -> TradingEdge.Scraper) carries no release timestamp, and minimumReleaseAgeBehaviour's
    // default (timestamp-required) treats that as "not old enough" forever -- it sat in
    // "Pending Status Checks" from 10-18 with no branch and no PR. The weekend window still
    // applies; only the 3-day wait is lifted. Both package-name shapes: "owner/repo" (actions,
    // github-tags) and the URL form (git-refs, uv git sources).
    {
      matchPackageNames: ["/^(https://github\\.com/)?mjdevaccount//i"],
      minimumReleaseAge: null,
    },
  ],
}
